    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "At the start of this episode, Chef Barinov feeds his fish Arkady before welcoming Alexey Smolin, a Michelin inspector visiting the restaurant. Even after an awkward spill, the inspector praises the chef's signature tasting menu.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Не смотри на меня так.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Тебя нельзя сейчас перекормить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "Здравствуйте, Виктор Петрович.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "Я инспектирую ваш ресторан.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "С вами всё в порядке?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "Да, просто так неожиданно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Здравствуйте, мне очень приятно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "Ваш авторский сет просто отличный.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "Он закрыл этот неприятный случай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Спасибо вам за добрые слова.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вы только не волнуйтесь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мы всё быстро отстираем.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Не смотри на меня так.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do not look at me like that.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'not' before landing on 'so'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Тебя нельзя сейчас перекормить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You must not be overfed right now.' The meaning hinges on 'you' working together with 'to overfeed' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "Здравствуйте, Виктор Петрович.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hello, Viktor Petrovich.' The meaning hinges on 'hello' working together with 'hello' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "Я инспектирую ваш ресторан.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am inspecting your restaurant.' the inspector brings out the contrast between 'I' and 'I inspect' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "С вами всё в порядке?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Is everything alright with you?' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'with' and finishing on 'order'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "Да, просто так неожиданно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, it is just so unexpected.' Notice how Russian expresses 'yes' and 'unexpectedly' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Здравствуйте, мне очень приятно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hello, I am very pleased to meet you.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'hello' before landing on 'pleasant'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "Ваш авторский сет просто отличный.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Your signature set is simply excellent.' The grammar packs 'your' and 'excellent' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "Он закрыл этот неприятный случай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It overshadowed that unpleasant incident.' Notice how Russian expresses 'he' and 'case' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Спасибо вам за добрые слова.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Thank you for the kind words.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'thank you' before landing on 'words'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вы только не волнуйтесь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Just do not worry.' The negative particle sits right before the verb meaning 'worry' to negate the statement.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мы всё быстро отстираем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We will wash everything out quickly.' In Russian, the idea of 'we' flows naturally into 'we will wash out' without extra filler words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
